<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Developer ID distribution (NOT App Store sandbox).  The Bridge dlopen's third-party
    dylibs at runtime — libndi (NDI Runtime) and libsrt.  Under the Hardened Runtime that
    notarization requires, dlopen of a library signed by a different Team ID is BLOCKED
    unless library validation is disabled.  Without this, NDI and SRT silently fail on a
    notarized build even though they work in debug.  No sandbox key → full network/file
    access for a pro LAN tool.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Lets the app install its bundled Virtual Camera system extension.  This is a
    RESTRICTED entitlement: it only takes effect when the signature carries a
    provisioning profile that grants the System Extension capability, and macOS refuses
    to load the extension unless the app runs from /Applications.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.developer.system-extension.install</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    A shared container that carries NOTHING.  Frames reach the extension through its
    CoreMediaIO SINK stream, never through here — an App Group cannot carry them, because
    macOS runs a camera extension as its own user and the same group path resolves to two
    different places on the two sides (see CMIOSinkConnection).

    Kept anyway, and deliberately: the pair of entitlements is part of the signature both
    sides were approved under, and this feature has cost enough approval-and-restart cycles
    that changing the signing shape to delete an unused key is not a trade worth making on
    a release build.  Remove it in a quiet window, on its own, with a full approve-and-open
    test — never bundled with something else.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
  <array>
    <string>XWBJP49FTR.studio.airlive.bridge</string>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
